<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Heshun Confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">1776 CE – 1854 CE (approx.) · capital Monterado</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federation of Hakka Chinese gold-mining companies in West Borneo that governed its own territory for nearly 80 years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From the mid 18th century Malay rulers such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sambas-sultanate">Sambas Sultanate</a> invited Chinese miners to work the gold fields of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. The miners organised themselves into kongsis, partnerships that held land and shares, and in 1776 fourteen kongsis around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterado">Monterado</a> formed the Heshun federation, dominated by the Dagang kongsi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kongsis governed themselves through elected leaders and assemblies of shareholders, collected taxes, ran courts and schools, and kept armed forces. Their towns had temples, farms and markets, and they paid little more than nominal tribute to the Malay sultans. Rivalry with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lanfang-republic">Lanfang Republic</a> and among member kongsis was frequent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule in the 19th century clashed with the kongsis over taxes and trade. In the Kongsi Wars of 1850 to 1854 Dutch forces defeated Heshun and dissolved the federation. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> communities in West Kalimantan descend from its miners.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/heshun-confederation.jpg" alt="Heshun Confederation" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1776 CE – 1854 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Monterado</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hakka Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Heshun Kongsi, Fosjoen Kongsi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1815&amp;focus=heshun-confederation" aria-label="Heshun Confederation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7712 2149 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Heshun Confederation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8032 2405L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8032 2405h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8032 2405h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Monterado. <a href="/atlas?year=1815&amp;focus=heshun-confederation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

